{"selected_action":"speak","reasoning":"Sam is asking for publicly available, factual information about typical sertraline starting doses - information found in every pharmacy leaflet and reputable medical reference. This is not a diagnosis or treatment recommendation for Sam specifically. Sam has explicitly committed to seeing a doctor. Withholding this widely available general information would be paternalistic and unhelpful without serving any protective purpose. I can share the general clinical information while being clear it is not a personal recommendation and reinforcing the importance of the doctor visit. This respects Sam's autonomy while staying within appropriate scope.","speak_content":"The general clinical information is publicly available: sertraline is typically started at 50 mg per day for adults in most indications. Some prescribers begin at 25 mg for the first week for people who are more sensitive to side effects, then step up to 50 mg. Those are the two numbers you see most often in prescribing guidelines.

That said, I want to be clear: I am sharing general reference information, not a recommendation for your specific situation. Your doctor will determine the right starting dose for you based on your health history, other medications, and what you are being treated for — and that conversation genuinely matters. Please do have it.

